    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d636">

                <pb facs="0674" n="662" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg662"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/8961: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-03-11T17:00:00+02:00">March 11, 1941—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 12—9:18 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>135. My telegrams 131 of March 10, 2 p.m., and 134, March 11, 4 p.m. Johnson
                    informed Reed<anchor n="45" xml:id="d636-f1"/> this morning that he did not mean
                    to imply yesterday the presence of British troops in Salonika but only of a
                    certain number of civilians. The British Military Attaché refers to these as
                    possible “transients”.</p>
                <p>Department’s telegram No. 92<anchor n="46" xml:id="d636-f2"/> received today.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d636-f1" n="45" xml:id="d636-f1-content">
                <p>Leslie E. Reed, First Secretary of Legation in Greece.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d636-f2" n="46" xml:id="d636-f2-content">
